{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/assets/365c6e6ebc861b9d8276773d5afe39b971096f0de6f07fced2c5a2ea9e035554.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{r}^{3}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1f6647af3c35cd22ab19fb950fb2cce063422d4b91297352849fea33d60ec73"} {"question" "q-161146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "12\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "8\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/assets/9795c04bf6d4bfa407a7b9176cd5c4b97b3d89bfcc6fff17d1d78190047b3a54.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1f6647af3c35cd22ab19fb950fb2cce063422d4b91297352849fea33d60ec73"} {"question" "q-161291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/assets/5ba2dbc746b9fe8b36202d31bbeb8193ffb9e747d6ed4c2e2f0f994499f88e8e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-x+\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the second quadrant, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1f6647af3c35cd22ab19fb950fb2cce063422d4b91297352849fea33d60ec73"} {"question" "q-161311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4(x+y)\\,\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4xy+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[2{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1f6647af3c35cd22ab19fb950fb2cce063422d4b91297352849fea33d60ec73"} {"question" "q-161341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}-1"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}(1+x+y)^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}\\,\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(-\\frac{1}{1+x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+x]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1f6647af3c35cd22ab19fb950fb2cce063422d4b91297352849fea33d60ec73"} {"question" "q-161353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d91ac3ebe7c0577bb23ff39da9e6dc63d2"} {"question" "q-161353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/state.json" "file_sha256" "944b318c6ddd494af497d0b7971f44d9